WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:03.240
If you're hiking pretty
much anywhere in the US,

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:06.613
this is one sound you don't want to hear.

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:11.270
The warning of a rattlesnake.

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:13.190
Now, just because a rattlesnake's tail

00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:15.410
sounds like a built-in maraca

00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:17.280
doesn't mean it works like one.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:19.710
There are no beads rattling about in here.

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:21.813
So, what's really going on inside?

00:00:23.030 --> 00:00:26.070
If you opened up a rattlesnake's
rattle and shook it,

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:28.570
absolutely nothing would fall out.

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:29.403
After all...

00:00:29.403 --> 00:00:32.090
Tim Colston: So, rattlesnake
rattles are hollow.

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:34.480
Narrator: That's
herpetologist Tim Colston.

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:36.950
He says the secret to that rattling sound

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:39.330
comes from the shell itself.

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:40.550
It's made of keratin,

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:43.563
the same hard substance
as your fingernails.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:49.030
The keratin is arranged in a
chain of interlocking rings,

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:53.020
which are hooked together by
tiny grooves along their edge.

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:54.950
Now, watch what happens to those rings

00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:56.913
when Colston shakes the rattle.

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:01.360
Colston: Whenever I shake them very fast,

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:03.920
they bump together, producing the sound.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:06.020
Narrator: Because the
rattle is hollow inside,

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:08.540
sound waves can bounce
off the walls and echo,

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:12.170
the same way shouting in a
cave amplifies the sound.

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:15.340
And the bigger the cave, or
hollow rings in this case,

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:19.010
the more amplification,
so the louder the rattle.

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:23.090
But a big, hollow chamber can't
get the job done on its own.

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:25.460
That's where the tail muscles come in.

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:28.800
Rattlesnakes are equipped with
three powerful shaker muscles

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.690
at the base of their spine.

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:33.000
These can contract so fast,

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.990
they vibrate the rattle
up to 90 times a second.

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:39.020
For comparison, the human eye blinks

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:41.400
15 to 20 times a minute.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:42.930
By vibrating so quickly,

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:44.450
the rattle makes a sound that hits

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:47.360
between these specific frequencies.

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:49.700
And it just so happens that that range

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:51.660
is best heard by mammals.

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:53.910
It turns out, that tail is custom designed

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:56.050
to make predators like bears, raccoons,

00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:58.130
and weasels listen up.

00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:00.428
Unfortunately, snakelets

00:02:00.428 --> 00:02:02.640
(yes, that's what baby snakes are called),

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:04.580
don't have this warning signal.

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:06.680
Colston: Whenever a rattlesnake is born,

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:09.660
it just has a single button.

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:11.250
It looks similar to the one,

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:13.410
I don't know if you can
see, that's on the end here.

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:15.890
Narrator: Without a second
button to clack against,

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:18.640
the baby rattle can't make any noise.

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:20.500
But every time they shed their skin,

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:23.210
they add another button
to the base of the rattle,

00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:26.160
which grows into the segment above,

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:29.460
sort of like the structure
of a Russian stacking doll.

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:33.170
The rattle will keep growing until...snap!

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:34.470
Just like your fingernails,

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:36.295
those rattles are pretty fragile,

00:02:36.295 --> 00:02:39.100
and they can break off
if they get too long.

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:42.900
In fact, the rattles rarely
make it past eight to 10 rings

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:44.840
before snapping off.

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:48.270
Luckily, snakes shed their entire lives.

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:51.270
So the rattle will grow back good as new,

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:53.130
which is good news for you hikers,

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:55.710
because that handy tail
can mean the difference

00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:58.660
between an exciting day on the trail

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:00.853
and a painful trip to the hospital.

